WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.280
A century ago the apocalyptic abounded and it&nbsp;
echoes in today's political discourse. Activist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.000
progressives have a history of apocalypticism&nbsp;
on issues like climate change, but now some on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.960
the political right have been captivated by these&nbsp;
very same impulses. The problem with apocalyptic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.920
politics is two-fold: it corrupts public life&nbsp;
by reducing the non-political complexity of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.560
to political warfare and secondly, it blinds&nbsp;
us to everything that's actually going well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.320
So what came of the apocalyptism of the 1920s?&nbsp;
Well in the wake of the great depression and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.440
World War II, imaginative thought leaders focused&nbsp;
on what was good and true in western civilization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.560
And America experienced the flowering of literary&nbsp;
cultural and economic progress. So what are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.040
good things hiding in plain sight today? From a&nbsp;
newfound awareness of the importance of marriage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.680
and faith, to American's strong sense of place&nbsp;
patriotism and belief in the American dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.400
to a robust embrace of education&nbsp;
innovation and medical breakthroughs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.000
It is the recoverists who will find each&nbsp;
other and defend the first principles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.680
practices, and institutions on which all of these&nbsp;
good things depend. So how worried are you about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.320
America's future let us know in our poll. Also let&nbsp;
us know what other topics you'd like our scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.400
to cover In 60 Seconds and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

